.\" Manpage for qnginxscanner.
.TH QNGINXSCANNER 1 "2026-11-18" "qnginxscanner 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
qnginxscanner \- detect nginx servers, fingerprint versions, flag misconfigurations and known vulnerabilities
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B qnginxscanner
[\fIoptions\fR] \fItarget\fR [\fItarget\fR ...]
.br
.B qnginxscanner
\fB\-\-web\fR [\fB\-\-listen\fR \fIhost:port\fR]
.br
.B qnginxscanner
\fB\-\-config\fR \fIfile.yaml\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B qnginxscanner
scans network ports and HTTP(S) services to decide, with auditable evidence,
whether they are served by nginx or an nginx derivative (OpenResty, Tengine,
Angie, freenginx, NGINX Plus, Kong, Apache APISIX), and produces machine output
and human-readable reports.
.PP
Detection does not depend on the Server banner alone. It combines the banner,
the built-in error-page template, default pages, nginx-specific messages, the
static-file ETag format, the wire order of response headers, nginx-only reason
phrases, the HTTP/2 SETTINGS fingerprint, exposed status endpoints,
nginx-related headers and cookies, and the mail proxy's default greetings.
Each signal has a weight; the weights add up to a confidence from 0 to 100
(\fBconfirmed\fR at 70 or more, \fBlikely\fR at 50, \fBpossible\fR at 25).
.PP
In
.B deep
mode, the default, it also fingerprints the version, release branch, protocols
and modules, checks for misconfigurations (exposed status and metrics pages,
exposed sensitive files, alias traversal, CRLF header injection, the PHP
path-info misconfiguration, directory listing), evaluates the release lifecycle, and
correlates the version against the nginx.org security advisories, including a
dedicated assessment of the NGINX2Root chain.
.PP
The tool is read-only. It never attempts to exploit anything and never sends
traffic meant to trigger a memory-safety bug.
.PP
It also discovers and parses a published RFC 9116
.I security.txt
and identifies a reverse proxy, CDN or WAF in front of a service.
.PP
Only scan hosts and networks you own or are explicitly authorised to test.
.SH TARGETS
Each target may be a URL
.RB ( https://example.com/ ),
an IPv4 or IPv6 literal
.RB ( 203.0.113.10 ,
.BR 2001:db8::1 ),
a CIDR block
.RB ( 10.0.0.0/24 ),
a dashed range
.RB ( 192.168.1.10-20 ,
.BR 10.0.0.1-10.0.0.50 ,
.BR 2001:db8::1-2001:db8::ff ),
a wildcard
.RB ( 192.168.1.* ,
.BR 10.0.*.* ,
.BR 2001:db8::* ),
a DNS wildcard
.RB ( *.example.com ,
which enumerates subdomains via Certificate Transparency),
or a hostname.
A URL target's scheme, port and path are honoured and override the port set.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Scan selection
.TP
.BI \-\-mode " deep|detect"
Scan depth. \fBdeep\fR (default) decides whether a service is nginx and
additionally fingerprints version, branch, protocols and modules, reports
findings, and correlates vulnerabilities including NGINX2Root. \fBdetect\fR
is the lighter mode: it only decides whether a service is nginx, with a
handful of requests per service.
.TP
.BI \-\-ports " spec"
Ports to check: a single port, a comma-separated list, inclusive ranges, or a
mixture (e.g. \fB80,443,8000-8100\fR). Default: a built-in common-web-port set
(80, 443, 8000, 8008, 8080, 8443, 8888, 3000, 5000, 9000).
.TP
.BI \-\-tls " auto|on|off"
TLS handling. \fBauto\fR (default) infers from the port and falls back to the
other scheme; \fBon\fR forces HTTPS; \fBoff\fR forces HTTP.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-raw
Skip the raw-request probes and the HTTP/2 connection-preface fingerprint, so
that only well-formed HTTP requests are sent. Header order, reason phrases and
the HTTP/2 fingerprint are then not available as evidence.
.TP
.B \-\-banners
Grab a short service banner from open ports that do not speak HTTP. This also
detects the nginx mail proxy from its default POP3, IMAP and SMTP greetings.
.TP
.B \-\-follow
Follow HTTP redirects while collecting evidence (default \fBtrue\fR).
.TP
.B \-\-insecure
Do not verify TLS certificates.
.TP
.B \-\-security\-txt
Look up and parse /.well-known/security.txt (default \fBtrue\fR).
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout " duration"
Per-connection timeout, e.g. \fB5s\fR or \fB500ms\fR (default \fB10s\fR).
.SS Targets
.TP
.BI \-\-targets\-file " file" ", " \-iL " file"
Read targets from a file, one per line (\- for stdin). Blank lines and
comments starting with # are ignored.
.TP
.BI \-\-allow " pattern"
Keep only targets whose host matches \fIpattern\fR (an exact host/IP, a CIDR
block, or a \fB*.domain\fR / \fB.domain\fR suffix). Repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-block " pattern"
Drop targets whose host matches \fIpattern\fR (same forms as \fB\-\-allow\fR).
Repeatable; applied after the allow list.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-hosts " n"
Maximum hosts a single target spec may expand to.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-dns\-enum
Disable subdomain enumeration for \fB*.domain\fR targets.
.TP
.B \-\-verify\-subdomains
Keep only enumerated subdomains that resolve in DNS.
.TP
.B \-\-resolve\-dns
Record forward and reverse (PTR) DNS for each host.
.SS Output
.TP
.BI \-\-format " text|json|xml|csv|sarif|ndjson"
Machine-readable scan output format (default \fBtext\fR).
.TP
.BI \-\-out " file"
Write scan output to \fIfile\fR instead of standard output. The extension
(json, xml or txt) selects the format unless \fB\-\-format\fR is given.
.TP
.BI \-\-report " text|rtf|pdf|html"
Also produce a human-readable report in the given format.
.TP
.BI \-\-report\-out " file"
Write the report to \fIfile\fR (required for \fBrtf\fR, \fBpdf\fR and
\fBhtml\fR). Without \fB\-\-report\fR, a .pdf or .rtf extension selects that
format; anything else is written as text.
.TP
.BI \-\-oA " base"
Write json/xml/csv/sarif/txt outputs and an HTML report to \fIbase\fR.*
.TP
.B \-\-include\-closed
Include closed/filtered ports in the output.
.TP
.BI \-\-fail\-on " level"
Exit with status 2 if the result meets \fIlevel\fR.
\fBlow\fR, \fBmedium\fR, \fBhigh\fR, \fBcritical\fR: a vulnerability lead or
finding is at least that severe.
\fBeol\fR: an nginx service is on an end-of-life branch.
\fBnginx2root\fR: an nginx service runs a version in the affected range of the
NGINX2Root advisories. The gates use deep-mode data.
.TP
.BI \-\-diff " file"
Compare against a prior JSON scan result and print the differences.
.TP
.BI \-\-diff\-format " text|json"
Format for \fB\-\-diff\fR output (default text).
.TP
.B \-\-verbose ", " \-v
Print a line per host/port as it is probed (open/closed, nginx verdict,
version), plus subdomain-enumeration steps.
.TP
.B \-\-quiet ", " \-q
Suppress progress output.
.SS Politeness
.TP
.BI \-\-concurrency " n"
Maximum concurrent service probes (default \fB50\fR).
.TP
.BI \-\-rate " n"
Cap outbound requests to n per second across the scan.
.TP
.BI \-\-per\-host\-concurrency " n"
Limit concurrent probes against a single host.
.TP
.BI \-\-host\-concurrency " n"
Limit how many distinct hosts are scanned at once (0 = no host-level cap).
.TP
.BI \-\-jitter " duration"
Sleep a random time up to this before each service probe.
.TP
.BI \-\-retries " n"
Retry transient network failures n times with backoff.
.TP
.BI \-\-request\-budget " n"
Cap the optional deep-mode probes per service (status endpoints, the CRLF
check, the sensitive-file checks, the alias and path-info checks); they stop once the budget is spent (0 = no cap). A
host returning 429/503 skips the optional probes altogether.
.SS Request shaping
.TP
.BI \-\-header " 'Name: value'"
Add a request header (repeatable).
.TP
.BI \-\-cookie " value"
Send a Cookie header.
.TP
.BI \-\-bearer " token"
Send Authorization: Bearer token.
.TP
.BI \-\-basic " user:pass"
HTTP Basic authentication.
.TP
.BI \-\-vhost " host"
Override the Host header (virtual-host scanning).
.TP
.BI \-\-user\-agent " string"
Override the HTTP User-Agent header.
.TP
.BI \-\-proxy " url"
Route requests through an http(s):// or socks5:// proxy. The raw-request and
HTTP/2 fingerprint probes need a direct connection and are skipped.
.TP
.BI \-\-resolve " host:port:addr"
Pin
.I host:port
to dial
.I addr
instead (curl \-\-resolve style), keeping the URL host for the Host header and
TLS SNI. Use it to reach an origin behind a CDN. Repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-sni " name"
Override the TLS SNI server name, independent of the Host header. A name that
does not match the presented certificate fails verification unless
.B \-\-insecure
is set.
.SS Data
.TP
.BI \-\-vuln\-feed " file|url"
Merge an external vulnerability feed (repeatable). The native
\fBqnginxscanner-vuln-feed/1\fR schema, OSV records (single or array) and CSAF
2.0 advisories are auto-detected; from OSV and CSAF only nginx packages are
kept. Entries are added to the built-in catalogue; a native feed may also
replace the set of supported branches and their latest releases. With
\fB\-\-web\fR, the feeds are merged into the catalogue used by every scan
started from the web GUI and by its origin comparison.
.TP
.BI \-\-signature\-pack " file|url"
Load a versioned detection-signature pack (JSON, schema
\fBqnginxscanner-signature-pack/1\fR) adding response-header, cookie-name and
response-body matchers to the built-in catalogues. Regular expressions are
length-bounded and validated; weights are clamped. The active packs are
recorded in the scan's provenance metadata. Repeatable, and honoured by the
web GUI when passed at start-up.
.SS Audit, resume and replay
.TP
.BI \-\-resume " file"
Checkpoint completed services to \fIfile\fR and resume from it.
.TP
.BI \-\-pcap " file"
Write a libpcap capture of every HTTP exchange to \fIfile\fR, reconstructing
each request/response as a TCP conversation for an audit trail. The file opens
in Wireshark, tcpdump or tshark.
.TP
.BI \-\-log " file"
Write a human-readable session log to \fIfile\fR recording each request and,
per service, how every piece of evidence and each verdict was decided.
.TP
.BI \-\-record\-corpus " file"
Record a sanitised fixture of the scan's detection inputs (the responses fed
to analysis) to \fIfile\fR for offline replay. Cookie values and credential
headers are redacted; response bodies are kept verbatim, so review a corpus
before sharing it.
.TP
.BI \-\-replay\-corpus " file"
Replay a corpus recorded with \fB\-\-record\-corpus\fR back through detection
offline, with no network and no targets, emitting a scan result as a live run
would. Honours \fB\-\-vuln\-feed\fR, \fB\-\-signature\-pack\fR,
\fB\-\-format\fR, \fB\-\-report\fR, \fB\-\-diff\fR and \fB\-\-fail\-on\fR.
.SS General
.TP
.BI \-\-config " file"
Load settings from a YAML configuration file. Explicit flags override config
values; positional targets override the config's target list.
.TP
.B \-\-web
Run the web GUI instead of a one-shot scan.
.TP
.BI \-\-listen " host:port"
Address for the web GUI (default \fB127.0.0.1:8088\fR).
.TP
.BI \-\-web\-auth " user:pass"
Protect the web GUI with HTTP Basic authentication.
.TP
.BI \-\-completion " shell"
Print a shell completion script for \fIshell\fR (bash, zsh or fish) and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print version information and exit.
.SH PROBES
Every probe is read-only. Deep is the default depth, so a scan without
.B \-\-mode
sends everything listed here;
.B \-\-mode detect
sends only the handful of requests in the next paragraph.
.PP
In both modes, each open port receives: a GET of the target path; a GET of
.IR /qnginxscanner_probe_404 ,
a guaranteed-missing path that yields the server's own error page; a raw
HTTP/1.1 request without a Host header; on TLS services, a cleartext HTTP
request to the TLS port; and the security.txt lookups.
.PP
Deep mode adds: an OPTIONS request; a raw request with an unknown method, one
with a 9000-byte URI and one
.RB ( foreign\-host )
with the Host header
.IR qnginxscanner\-probe.invalid ;
the HTTP/2 connection preface (the server's SETTINGS
and WINDOW_UPDATE frames are read, no request is made); GETs of
.IR /nginx_status ", " /stub_status ", " /basic_status ", " /nginx\-status ", "
.IR /status ", " /server\-status ", " /api/ ", " /status/format/json " and " /metrics ;
a CRLF header-injection check whose path contains an encoded CR LF and a marker
header; GETs of
.IR /.git/HEAD ", " /.htpasswd ", " /.htaccess ", " /.env " and " /nginx.conf
(the content is validated but never copied into the report); and the alias
off-by-slash and PHP path-info checks, which re-request up to three static
assets the page already links to (four requests each) through
.IR /seg../seg/file ,
a control path, and
.IR /seg/file/qnginxscanner.php .
.PP
.B \-\-no\-raw
removes the raw requests and the HTTP/2 preface. They are also skipped when
.B \-\-proxy
is set.
.SH FINDINGS
Deep mode reports these behaviour-based findings, all from read-only requests:
.BR git\-exposed " (high, /.git/HEAD is served),"
.BR htpasswd\-exposed " (high, /.htpasswd),"
.BR env\-exposed " (high, /.env),"
.BR php\-pathinfo " (high),"
.BR alias\-traversal " (high),"
.BR crlf\-injection " (medium),"
.BR plus\-api\-exposed " (medium),"
.BR nginx\-conf\-exposed " (medium, /nginx.conf),"
.BR stub\-status\-exposed " (low),"
.BR vts\-status\-exposed " (low),"
.BR metrics\-exposed " (low, Prometheus metrics at /metrics),"
.BR htaccess\-exposed " (low, /.htaccess),"
.BR host\-header\-redirect " (low),"
.BR autoindex\-enabled " (low),"
.BR version\-disclosure " (info),"
.BR default\-page " (info, the stock welcome page is served)."
.PP
.B php\-pathinfo
is the nginx + PHP-FPM path-info misconfiguration: a public static asset
requested as
.I asset/qnginxscanner.php
comes back with the same bytes as text/html, so static files are handed to PHP
and an uploaded file could execute as code.
.B host\-header\-redirect
means the foreign-host raw request was answered by a redirect whose Location
carries the probe's Host: redirects are built from $host or $http_host. It is
not checked with
.B \-\-no\-raw
or
.BR \-\-proxy .
nginx ignores .htaccess files, so the rules in an exposed one are not enforced,
and unlike Apache it does not protect .ht* files or dotfiles by default. The
content of an exposed file is validated but never copied into the report.
.SH NGINX2ROOT
NGINX2Root (Xint, November 2026, https://nginx2root.com/) comprises
CVE-2026-123007 (HTTP/2 flow-control memory corruption, nginx 1.9.5 to 1.31.8),
CVE-2026-123633 (HTTP/2 use-after-free, 1.19.7 to 1.31.8),
CVE-2026-121810 (HTTP/3 use-after-free, 1.25.0 to 1.31.8),
CVE-2026-123634 (resolver use-after-free, 0.6.18 to 1.31.8) and
CVE-2026-113425 (mail proxy use-after-free, 0.5.33 to 1.31.8).
All are fixed in nginx 1.31.9 (mainline) and 1.30.7 (stable).
.PP
In deep mode each nginx service is given one of three statuses:
.B vulnerable\-version
(the detected version is in an affected range),
.B not\-affected
(the detected version carries the fixes), or
.B unknown\-version
(the version is hidden, or the product uses its own version numbering).
The result also states whether HTTP/2, HTTP/3 and the mail proxy were observed
on that endpoint.
.PP
This is version-based detection. The tool does not attempt to trigger the
bugs, a hidden version cannot be assessed, and distribution packages may carry
backported fixes under an unchanged version number. NGINX Plus users should
consult F5 article K000164058.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The scan completed.
.TP
.B 1
A fatal error occurred (invalid target or port spec, unreadable config, feed
or signature pack, or an I/O error).
.TP
.B 2
The scan completed but a \fB\-\-fail\-on\fR gate matched.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Deep scan (the default depth) of the common web ports:
.B qnginxscanner example.com
.TP
Only decide whether the service is nginx:
.B qnginxscanner \-\-mode detect example.com
.TP
Deep scan with a PDF report:
.B qnginxscanner \-\-report\-out report.pdf example.com
.TP
Fail a pipeline when any host runs an NGINX2Root-affected version:
.B qnginxscanner \-\-fail\-on nginx2root \-\-targets\-file hosts.txt
.TP
Look for the mail proxy as well as web services:
.B qnginxscanner \-\-banners \-\-ports 25,110,143,80,443 mail.example.com
.TP
Subnet sweep, HTTPS only, JSON to a file:
.B qnginxscanner \-\-tls on \-\-format json \-\-out scan.json 10.0.0.0/28
.TP
Enumerate and scan subdomains with per-host detail:
.B qnginxscanner \-\-verbose '*.example.com'
.TP
Deep scan with a full audit trail (PCAP capture and decision log):
.B qnginxscanner \-\-pcap scan.pcap \-\-log scan.log example.com
.TP
Launch the web GUI:
.B qnginxscanner \-\-web
.SH FILES
.TP
.I config.example.yaml
Annotated example configuration shipped with the source.
.TP
.I docs/vuln\-feed.example.json
Example vulnerability feed in the native schema.
.TP
.I docs/signature\-pack.example.json
Example detection-signature pack.
.SH SEE ALSO
Full documentation in
.IR docs/USAGE.md " and " docs/DESIGN.md .
The nginx security advisories: https://nginx.org/en/security_advisories.html
.SH CAVEATS
Vulnerability results are leads correlated from the detected version only, not
confirmed by exploitation; many require a specific protocol, module or
directive, which is reported as observed, contradicted or unknown. A server
that hides its version receives no leads. Tengine, Angie, freenginx, Kong and
Apache APISIX are detected but not correlated against nginx advisories. Always
verify against the live configuration before acting.
